00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.880
from module 6.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.000
Links to the videos and codelabs
are below so you can catch up,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.220
as we're going to pick up
where module 8 left off

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.310
and merge in part of
that module 6 migration.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.810
MARTIN OMANDER:
Sounds good, Wes.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.790
You know I'm here to
learn all about the ways

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.990
to modernize my apps and get
hands-on experience doing so.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.170
Can you put me to code,
as well as the codelab,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.948
so I can do this migration on
my own time after watching you?

00:02:34.948 --> 00:02:36.240
WESLEY CHUN: Of course, Martin.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.400
Same drill as before
for all of you.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.070
Use your module 8
solution or clone the repo

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.140
or download the zip file
if you don't have it.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.880
We'll start with
the module 8 code

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.970
and finish with what's in
the module 9 repo folder.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.300
Links to the repo and codelab
are down below, so pause here

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.130
if you need to go get anything.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:53.730
Ready?

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.765
Let's dive into
these migrations.

00:02:57.765 --> 00:03:00.140
Since we're doing several
migrations with a bunch of code

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.598
changes, you have
to confirm you're

00:03:01.598 --> 00:03:05.360
starting with the working module
8 code, whether yours or ours.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.000
If you're on Python 2,
double-check your lib folder

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.370
or blow it away and run
the pip install command

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.670
to reinstall those third-party
packages into the lib folder.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.310
Then use gcloud app deploy
and confirm your App Engine

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.850
app outputs the same most
recent visit list and possibly

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.280
older visits to delete
with a push task.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.898
Now, if things look good,
copy the entire folder,

00:03:24.898 --> 00:03:26.690
as we're going to make
major changes to it,

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.840
as we will deploy to a
completely different project.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.170
First up, app.yaml,
where modern App Engine

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.460
doesn't require much.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.920
For simple apps like this
one, the most critical part

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.710
is switching from Python
2 to 3, and as a result,

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:43.250
moving from the first generation
to second-generation service.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.260
One big change is
that web frameworks

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.670
must do their own routing.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.790
This means that all app
handlers have to switch to auto.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.620
But if everything
is auto, you really

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.210
don't need them
at all, especially

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.410
since the framework does it.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.660
So yes, you can remove all app
handlers, like what we did.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.990
But if you've got static
files, leave them here,

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.600
because that does not go
through the framework.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.270
Next, the section 4 built-in
third party libraries

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.350
is no longer needed.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.140
They now go into
requirements.txt.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.050
Similarly, there's no more
copying or self-bundling

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.790
of non-built-in libraries.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.720
You just put them into
requirements.txt and that's it.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.360
This is covered in a bit more
detail in the module 2 video,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.320
linked below.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.150
For our simple
app, the net result

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.000
is that you can delete all lines
except for the runtime, where

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.700
we're sending it
to python38 here.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.570
Since all third-party management
is in requirements.txt,

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.680
that means no more
appengine_config file,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.560
so delete that, too.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.890
For requirements.txt, we know
that all third-party packages

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:40.970
go in here now.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.850
Version numbers do need a
bump, because every library

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.460
has a final version
that works on Python 2.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.430
For us, this was
Cloud Tasks 1.5.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:52.670
Today, the version
for Python 3 is 2.3,

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.230
but that too will change.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.680
Similarly, we're switching from
Cloud NDB to Cloud Firestore,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.050
so this line has to
be completely swapped.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.470
In Python 2, we self-bundled
third-party libraries with pip

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.130
install.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.900
But in Python 3, don't do that.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.940
Seriously, Google
takes care of this

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.490
when deploying so you don't
have to lift a finger.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.350
Are we liking Python 3 yet?

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.360
Anyway, that's it
for the config files.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.140
Let's turn to the real
work, and that's in main.py.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.980
At the top, there
are two changes.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.680
One is that logging
in Python 3 is easier.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.880
You don't need to use Python
logging, just print to stdout.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.560
So remove that import.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.290
Next, we're switching from
Cloud NDB to Cloud Firestore,

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.700
so swap that client
library import.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.290
Finally, instantiate
the correct API clients.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.530
Next, all of the constants
for task queues stay the same,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.690
but we have to add a new one.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:45.110
The QUEUE_PATH variable is
the fully-qualified queue path

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:45.710
name.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.080
One difference between
App Engine task queues

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.290
and Cloud Tasks we didn't
cover last time in module 8

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.630
is that when using App
Engine push queues,

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.340
the default queue
was automatically

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.510
created by App Engine.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.000
But that's not the case
with Cloud Tasks, which

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.470
is independent of App Engine.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.290
With Cloud Tasks, you need to
create all of your push queues,

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.750
including the default
queue if you want one.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.900
And to do so, you need the path
name up to, but not including,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:09.770
the queue name.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.010
So that's what the
PATH_PREFIX is.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.440
For store_visit(), the changes
you see are the differences

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.840
between Cloud NDB
and Cloud Firestore.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.730
Cloud Firestore doesn't
have a data model,

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.490
nor does it require context
managers and with statements.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.180
In NDB, we refer to data records
as entities of a certain kind,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.970
while Firestore has documents
belonging to collections.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.100
Regardless of all this, know
that store_visit() has one job.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.060
It creates a new data record
with the connecting client IP,

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.130
as well as their web
client or user agent.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:38.090
One last note.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.010
NDB automatically
adds a timestamp

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.770
when an object is created,
but it must be done explicitly

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.650
in Firestore.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.870
Since App Engine task queues
create a default queue

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.180
by default, we didn't
need a function like

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.660
_create_queue_if() in
our module 8 main.py.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.420
Before creating a
task, this function

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.310
is used to check if
we need to create

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.630
the queue, which is only done
if it doesn't already exist.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.250
Next is fetch_visits().

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.250
At the top, querying for
the most recent visits

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.340
has to change from Cloud
NDB to Cloud Firestore.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.650
Again, the functionality
is identical.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:14.020
The differences are
just between how

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.630
NDB does it versus Firestore.

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:19.390
For the next section,
fortunately, Cloud Tasks

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.540
doesn't change between version
1 and version 2 of the client

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.030
library, so no real
updates to your code

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.920
are needed as to how
the task is created.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.790
Now the logging call
has to change to a print

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.730
and we have to add that check
to make sure the queue exists

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.770
before adding the task.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.450
You may ask why we
didn't do this before

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:38.830
with Cloud Tasks in module 8.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:42.790
And again, it's because the
module 7 code used App Engine

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:45.220
task queues, where the default
queue was automatically

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:46.490
created.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.250
Now, it wasn't
until this app where

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.980
we had to use Cloud Firestore,
requiring us to create

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.800
a completely brand new project,
that we discovered that we had

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.827
to make our own default queue.

00:07:55.827 --> 00:07:57.910
Let's skip over this new
function, _delete_docs(),

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.060
for just a moment and dive
into the trim() task handler.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.510
We saw just a moment ago from
fetch_visits() how querying is

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:08.140
identical, but NDB and Firestore
do it slightly differently.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:11.020
The same applies to this
query, looking for visits older

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.490
than the oldest one shown.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:15.850
One subtle difference is that
NDB has convenient batching,

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.940
like a keys-only query since I
don't need to look at the data,

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:22.060
plus a delete multi-function
for batch delete.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.090
In Firestore, you would issue
a regular query, then loop

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:28.570
through and delete them one at
a time or use a batch delete.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.510
Since I want to log all
the objects deleted,

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.539
I moved the deletion
loop into _delete_docs,

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:38.140
a generator that yields the ID
for each document deleted so I

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:42.010
can count how many were deleted
and log that back in trim().

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.770
Otherwise, I logged that
there weren't any old records

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:45.670
to delete.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.720
The main GET handler
doesn't change at all,

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.160
so we're skipping that.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.730
So overall, that
was probably one

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:54.610
of the more intense migrations
so far in this series

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.770
if you look at how
much code was deleted,

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.200
updated, or new code was added.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.870
Simple migrations are
great for teaching,

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.740
but generally not as realistic.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.880
So the goal was to try
and make it more lifelike.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.860
We actually forgot
one last migration

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.110
of porting this app
from Python 2 to 3.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.700
But don't worry, I'm
not going to keep

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.260
this video running too long.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.330
I deliberately crafted
all of the sample

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.110
apps to run on both Python 2
and 3 without any modifications

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.900
or compatibility libraries,
so the porting part

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.310
is already done.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.980
Now that everyone's on Python 3,
you've got no more pip install,

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.610
so just run gcloud app
deploy to confirm it

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:32.920
works as advertised,
making sure that you

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.680
change to your new
project before doing so.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.170
Because we're doing more
migrations in this module,

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:39.820
the chances of doing
something wrong

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.780
is higher, so let the logs
help you or you can check out

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.700
our solution in the
module 9 repo folder

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.780
to compare your
answers with ours.

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:48.280
All right.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:51.820
Let's go back to the
main presentation.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:54.550
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow, lots of
different migrations and things

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:55.515
to consider.

00:09:55.515 --> 00:09:57.640
If I wanted to get more
information about migrating

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:02.140
my App Engine app from Python
2 to 3, where should I look?

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:02.950
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:04.675
The best place is
the Python 2 to 3

00:10:04.675 --> 00:10:06.160
App Engine migration guide.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:07.990
It links to all the
individual pages

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:10.840
for migrating to Cloud
Datastore, Cloud Tasks, and all

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.448
the other standalone services.

00:10:12.448 --> 00:10:13.240
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.480
And same for Cloud NDB
and Cloud Firestore?

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.660
I'd like to review both to get
a better idea of the differences

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:20.960
between them.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:22.352
Do you have any links for them?

00:10:22.352 --> 00:10:23.560
WESLEY CHUN: Yep, sure thing.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:26.200
While we don't have a
formal Cloud NDB product

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.170
page, nor an NDB to
Firestore migration guide,

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.660
your best resources are
the client library docs

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:34.918
for both, as well as the
general Cloud Firestore docs.

00:10:34.918 --> 00:10:35.710
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:37.700
Yeah, those look really useful.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.510
Also, now that we're checking
for and creating our own task

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:42.460
queues, I need to
read up on those?

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:43.720
WESLEY CHUN: Gotcha.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.630
Well, all the links mentioned
here, plus the Cloud Task

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:49.690
client library and general docs,
are down below for your reading

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:50.273
pleasure.

00:10:50.273 --> 00:10:51.190
MARTIN OMANDER: Great.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:52.570
Thanks for those, Wes.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.050
Are we doing any more
task queue migrations?

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:56.800
WESLEY CHUN: Nope.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.350
Well, this is the last of
the task queue miniseries.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.800
Summarizing today's adventure,
we ported our app from Python 2

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:03.400
to 3.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.920
We moved from Cloud
NDB to Cloud Firestore,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.750
combining modules 3 and 6.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:11.030
And we revved up to the latest
Cloud Tasks client library,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:13.150
including adding new code
to create the default

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.030
queue, something we didn't
need before since we were still

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:18.730
running on our original project
where the App Engine task

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:21.970
queues system automatically
created that default queue.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:23.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:26.567
What other migrations should I
consider for my App Engine app?

00:11:26.567 --> 00:11:28.150
WESLEY CHUN: Well,
since we've already

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:31.330
done Cloud NDB, Cloud
Datastore, and Firestore,

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.530
you're done with all
the database stuff.

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.010
Now if you're thinking about
containerizing your App Engine

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:39.730
apps to run on Cloud Run, well,
check out modules 4 and 5.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:41.470
Finally, if you've
got a tiny app that's

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.450
just one function
or a giant app you

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.280
want to break up
into microservices,

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.250
those sound like really good
use cases for Cloud Functions,

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:51.010
and that migration is
covered in module 11.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:52.450
There are other
migrations coming,

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.580
so look out for blog
posts, as well as checking

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.613
the repo from time to time
for more migration content.

00:11:57.613 --> 00:11:58.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Thanks, Wes.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:11:59.775
I'll check those out.

00:11:59.775 --> 00:12:01.150
WESLEY CHUN: And
thank all of you

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.632
for sticking with us for all
these different migrations.

00:12:03.632 --> 00:12:05.590
This is Wesley, on behalf
of Martin and Porter,

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:07.780
and we'll see you at the
next "Migration Station"

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:09.940
or on another "Serverless
Expedition" soon.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.290
